th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/taylor-series.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/taylor-series.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the Taylor series of a real analytic function $f$ around $x = a$?","options":["$\\sum_{n = 0}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{f^{(n)}(a)}{n!}(x-a)^n$","$\\sum_{n = 0}^{k}\\dfrac{f^{(n)}(x)}{n!}(a-x)^n$","$f(a)+f'(x)+f''(x)+\\cdots$","$\\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty} f(a)^n(x-a)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"25d737b8a6f66c66364f68ac","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What changes in the Taylor series of $f$ when the centre moves from $0$ to $a$?","options":["Powers $(x-a)^n$, coefficients still $f^{(n)}(0)/n!$","Powers $(x+a)^n$, coefficients $f^{(n)}(a)/n!$","Powers $x^n-a^n$, coefficients $f^{(n)}(a)/n!$","Powers $(x-a)^n$, coefficients $f^{(n)}(a)/n!$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e63857700c84eac07d4266cd","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"In the Taylor series around $a$, what is the coefficient of $(x-a)^2$?","options":["$\\dfrac{f''(a)}{2!}$","$\\dfrac{f''(x)}{2!}$","$f''(a)$","$\\dfrac{f'(a)}{2!}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2d8e77f082f704cac55489fe","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which is the Maclaurin series of $\\mathrm{e}^x$?","options":["$\\sum_{n = 0}^{\\infty} n!x^n$","$1-x+\\dfrac{x^2}{2!}-\\cdots$","$\\sum_{n = 0}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{x^n}{n!}$","$\\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{x}{n!}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7d48ead2e490c8cea9ac6a5b","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does real analyticity at $x = a$ guarantee in the proposition?","options":["That $f$ agrees with its Taylor series on the whole domain $E$.","That $f$ agrees with its Taylor series on some interval $(a-r,a+r)$.","That the Taylor series converges near $a$, possibly to another function.","That $f$ is a polynomial on some interval $(a-r,a+r)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c601dc55461afeb43376d252","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"When does a formal Taylor series necessarily converge to the original function?","options":["Every smooth function equals its Taylor series near every point of its domain.","A smooth function equals its Taylor series wherever that series converges near $a$.","It equals $f$ whenever the coefficients $f^{(n)}(a)/n!$ tend to $0$.","A smooth function has a formal Taylor series; it equals $f$ only if $f$ is analytic."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ed6347336a1df77f9a289ed8","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"In the generalized binomial expansion, what is the coefficient of $x^2$ in $(1+x)^r$?","options":["$r^2$","$\\dfrac{r(r+1)}{2}$","$\\dfrac{r(r-1)}{2}$","$r(r-1)(r-2)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"029aa3c1d6a61c9352a8bc09","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $f\\colon\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be the smooth function with $f(0)=0$ and $f(x)=\\mathrm{e}^{-1/x^2}$ for $x\\ne0$. All its derivatives at $0$ vanish. Which statement about its Taylor series at $0$ is correct?","options":["The series diverges at nonzero $x$ because $f(x)>0$ there.","The series equals $f$ near $0$ because $f$ has derivatives of every order.","The series is identically $0$, so it does not represent $f$ on any neighbourhood of $0$.","The series equals $f$ near $0$ because only finitely many coefficients are nonzero."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"db031af5256da98f06b3f9ea","time_limit":50},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is the condition that $a$ is an interior point relevant to the statement?","options":["It forces $E$ to be an open interval of the form $(a-r,a+r)$.","It guarantees the Taylor series converges at every $x\\in E$.","It ensures $f$ is real analytic at $a$ without further conditions.","It gives an interval $(a-r,a+r)\\subseteq E$ for the expansion."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3d7d221f08e92740af6259b1","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which expression gives the first three nonzero terms of the Maclaurin series for $\\mathrm{e}^x$?","options":["$1+x+x^2$.","$1-x+\\dfrac{x^2}{2}$.","$x+\\dfrac{x^2}{2}+\\dfrac{x^3}{6}$.","$1+x+\\dfrac{x^2}{2}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3d66dd24ac038619f6b0304d","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f$ be real analytic at $a=2$, with $f(2)=3$, $f'(2)=0$ and $f''(2)=4$. What is the linear term, possibly zero, of its Taylor series about $2$?","options":["$3(x-2)$.","$0\\cdot(x-2)$.","$2(x-2)^2$.","$4(x-2)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"70ccc9bd83351d599aa9b1ce","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the Taylor series of $\\ln x$ about $x=1$?","options":["$\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{(x-1)^n}{n!}$","$\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{(x-1)^n}{n}$","$\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{x^n}{n}$","$\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty\\frac{(x-1)^n}{n}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"23b93d24dc88530d9f08ae92","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_015_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which hypothesis on $f$ guarantees that the Taylor series of $f$ at $a$ converges to $f(x)$ for every $x$ in some interval around $a$?","options":["$f$ is real analytic at $a$.","$f$ has derivatives of every order on an interval around $a$.","$f$ is differentiable on an interval around $a$.","$f$ is continuous and bounded on an interval around $a$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"402c3356437daa20040e5ccc","time_limit":40}]}}